with q as (
  select $l14_quiz$[{"type": "mcq", "prompt": "The atmosphere degrades astronomical light in two fundamentally different ways. What is the key distinction between them?", "options": ["One dims light and the other brightens it; both are fixed by bigger mirrors", "Absorption/scattering BLOCKS whole wavelength bands (deciding WHAT you can see), while turbulence/seeing BLURS the light that gets through (deciding HOW SHARPLY) \u2014 and only seeing can be fought from the ground", "Both are the same effect measured in different units", "One affects radio and the other affects only visible light"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Absorption and scattering block light selectively by wavelength, deciding which parts of the spectrum reach the ground at all \u2014 the atmospheric windows. If the air absorbs a wavelength, it simply never arrives, and the only fix is to leave the atmosphere. Turbulence ('seeing') blurs even the transmitted wavelengths by corrugating the incoming wavefront, degrading resolution. Crucially, absorption cannot be corrected from the ground (space is the only option for a blocked band), whereas seeing can be partly corrected by adaptive optics or mitigated by high, stable sites. Absorption decides what you can see; turbulence decides how sharply.", "id": "q1"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is the James Webb Space Telescope \u2014 and all X-ray astronomy \u2014 necessarily done from space, while radio and optical astronomy stay on the ground?", "options": ["Space telescopes are always sharper, so everything eventually moves to orbit", "The atmosphere transmits the radio and optical windows but BLOCKS the infrared (water vapor) and completely absorbs UV, X-rays, and gamma rays (ozone and air) \u2014 so light in the blocked bands physically cannot reach the surface", "Radio and optical telescopes are too heavy to launch", "X-rays travel faster than radio waves and outrun the atmosphere"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The atmospheric transmission map sorts all astronomy into ground and space. Radio (~cm to ~10 m) and optical (~300\u20131100 nm) are open windows, done from the ground. Most of the infrared is blocked by water vapor (and the warm sky glows in the IR), which is why JWST \u2014 an infrared observatory chasing the redshifted first galaxies \u2014 had to go to space and to the cold of L2. UV is absorbed by ozone, and X-rays and gamma rays by the air itself, so not a single such photon reaches the ground: there is no mountain high enough. This is why Riccardo Giacconi had to fly a rocket above the air in 1962 to discover the first cosmic X-ray source, Scorpius X-1. For blocked bands, space is not better \u2014 it is the only option.", "id": "q2"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "On a normal night, an 8-meter ground telescope resolves no better than a ~15-centimeter one, even though its optics could do 60\u00d7 better. What is going on?", "options": ["The big mirror is defective and needs polishing", "Turbulence keeps the incoming wavefront flat only over patches ~r\u2080 (the Fried parameter, ~15 cm), so resolution is capped at \u03bb/r\u2080 regardless of the mirror's diameter \u2014 the telescope is 'seeing-limited'", "Bigger mirrors always resolve worse than small ones", "The star is simply too faint to resolve"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A telescope reaches its diffraction limit \u03b8 \u2248 \u03bb/D only if the wavefront is flat across its full aperture D. But atmospheric turbulence corrugates the wavefront, keeping it coherent only over patches of size r\u2080 \u2248 10\u201320 cm (the Fried parameter). So the effective aperture for resolution is r\u2080, not D, and the resolution is pinned at \u03bb/r\u2080 \u2248 1 arcsecond no matter how big the mirror. This is what 'seeing-limited' means: beyond ~15 cm of aperture, a bigger ground telescope gains only LIGHT (collecting area \u221d D\u00b2), not sharpness. It is the central limitation of ground-based astronomy, and the reason for both space telescopes and adaptive optics.", "id": "q3"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "The 2.4-meter Hubble delivers far sharper images than an 8-meter ground telescope on a normal night. How is the smaller telescope sharper \u2014 and what does the bigger one still win?", "options": ["Hubble has better glass; the ground telescope wins nothing", "Hubble is in vacuum, so it reaches its diffraction limit (~0.05\u2033) while the ground telescope is stuck at the ~1\u2033 seeing limit; but the 8-meter still collects ~11\u00d7 more light (area \u221d D\u00b2), winning on faint-object sensitivity", "Hubble is closer to the stars", "The ground telescope is sharper; the premise is false"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Resolution depends on aperture only if the wavefront is clean. Hubble's wavefront is clean (vacuum, no atmosphere), so it achieves its 0.058\u2033 diffraction limit; the ground telescope's wavefront is corrupted by turbulence, so it is stuck near 1\u2033 \u2014 far worse than either mirror's diffraction limit. Hubble wins on sharpness not because its mirror is better but because there is no atmosphere above it. But light-gathering scales as collecting area (D\u00b2): the 8-meter gathers (8/2.4)\u00b2 \u2248 11\u00d7 more photons, so it is far more powerful for detecting faint objects. This is the fundamental trade \u2014 the space telescope wins sharpness, the ground giant wins light \u2014 which is why both exist and are complementary. (With adaptive optics the ground telescope can recover its own 0.017\u2033 diffraction limit and beat Hubble.)", "id": "q4"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Adaptive optics recovers sharp images from the ground. Which statement best captures how it works and what it cannot do?", "options": ["It uses a bigger mirror to overpower the turbulence; it can see any wavelength", "It measures the corrugated wavefront ~1,000 times a second and bends a deformable mirror into the inverse shape to flatten it \u2014 but it cannot un-absorb blocked wavelengths (UV, X-ray, most IR), so space telescopes stay essential", "It heats the air to remove turbulence; it works only in daylight", "It slows the telescope's rotation to match the sky; it removes light pollution"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Adaptive optics senses the turbulence-corrugated wavefront with a wavefront sensor (using a bright natural star, or an artificial laser guide star made by exciting the 90-km sodium layer), then commands a deformable mirror into the inverse of the distortion so the reflected light comes out flat \u2014 running the sense-compute-actuate loop ~1,000 times per second because turbulence changes on millisecond timescales. When it works, the seeing-limited blob collapses to a near-diffraction-limited point (the technique behind the 2020 Nobel imaging of stars orbiting the galactic-center black hole). But AO fixes blur, not opacity: no wavefront correction un-absorbs a UV, X-ray, or water-vapor-absorbed IR photon, it corrects only a small patch of sky, and it doesn't remove sky glow. So AO wins back optical/near-IR sharpness, while space remains essential for the blocked spectrum. The atmosphere yields its blur but not its opacity.", "id": "q5"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the two ways the atmosphere degrades astronomical light, why they have different consequences, and how the resulting 'windows' map sorts all of astronomy into ground-based and space-based. Give specific examples of what goes where.", "rubric": "The two degradations: (1) ABSORPTION and scattering \u2014 the air's molecules (water vapor, ozone, oxygen, nitrogen, CO\u2082) absorb and scatter light selectively by wavelength, deciding WHICH parts of the spectrum reach the ground at all (the atmospheric windows); (2) REFRACTION/TURBULENCE ('seeing') \u2014 the air's refractive index varies with fluctuating temperature and density, blurring even the transmitted light, deciding HOW SHARPLY you can observe. Different consequences: absorption is an ABSOLUTE barrier \u2014 a blocked wavelength never reaches the surface, so it CANNOT be corrected from the ground and FORCES those observations to space; seeing is a degradation of transmitted light that CAN be fought from the ground (adaptive optics, high stable sites), so it merely ENCOURAGES space rather than requiring it. The windows map: OPEN windows are radio (~cm to ~10 m) and optical (~300\u20131100 nm) \u2014 done from the ground (radio dishes, optical observatories). PARTIAL: a few near-infrared bands at high, dry sites, but most infrared is blocked by water vapor (and the warm sky glows in the IR). BLOCKED entirely: ultraviolet (ozone), X-rays and gamma rays (the air itself) \u2014 space only. So the map sorts astronomy: radio astronomers stay on the ground; optical astronomers stay on the ground but fight the seeing; infrared astronomers climb high and dry or go to space (JWST is a space telescope precisely because it is an infrared observatory chasing redshifted early-galaxy light the ground cannot see); and UV, X-ray, and gamma-ray astronomers have no choice at all \u2014 all of high-energy astronomy is necessarily space-based (Chandra in X-rays; Giacconi's 1962 rocket that found Scorpius X-1 above the air). The location of every observatory ever built \u2014 ground or orbit \u2014 was decided first by which side of the atmospheric window its wavelength falls on. A strong answer notes the ozone irony (the layer that shelters skin from UV blinds UV astronomy) and that the atmosphere is a double barrier: it blocks some light entirely and blurs the rest.", "id": "q6"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain 'seeing' \u2014 from the plane wave to the Fried parameter \u2014 and why it drove even optical astronomy toward space and toward adaptive optics. Why do ground and space telescopes end up complementary rather than one replacing the other?", "rubric": "Seeing: a distant star's light reaches the top of the atmosphere as an essentially perfect PLANE WAVE (flat, parallel, because the star is a point at infinity); undisturbed, a telescope would focus it to a tiny diffraction-limited point. But crossing the TURBULENT atmosphere \u2014 cells of air at slightly different temperatures, hence densities, hence refractive indices, each bending light a little, all moving/changing on millisecond timescales \u2014 the wavefront arrives CORRUGATED and ever-changing, so the telescope cannot form a clean point; the star smears into a dancing, speckled blob (twinkling to the eye; ~1 arcsecond seeing to a telescope). The FRIED PARAMETER r\u2080 is the largest patch of the wavefront still reasonably flat at any instant (~10\u201320 cm in visible light at a good site). Consequence: resolution is capped at \u03bb/r\u2080, not \u03bb/D \u2014 the effective aperture for SHARPNESS is r\u2080, so an 8-m mirror resolves like a ~15-cm one (~1\u2033), degraded ~60\u00d7 from its 0.017\u2033 diffraction limit. 'Seeing-limited' means: beyond ~15 cm of aperture, a bigger ground telescope buys only LIGHT (area \u221d D\u00b2), not sharpness. Why it drove optical astronomy to space: since the atmosphere caps EVERY ground telescope at ~1\u2033 regardless of size, the only way to get a large mirror's full diffraction-limited sharpness is to remove the atmosphere \u2014 which is why the modest 2.4-m Hubble delivers ~0.05\u2033 images, ~20\u00d7 sharper than any ground telescope on a normal night, from vacuum alone (not a special mirror). So even in the transmitted optical window, seeing forces astronomy to space when sharpness matters most. Adaptive optics is the counterattack: measure the corrugation ~1,000\u00d7/s and cancel it with a deformable mirror (with laser guide stars where no natural star is near), recovering the diffraction limit from the ground (behind the 2020 Nobel galactic-center imaging; the basis of the coming 30\u201339 m ELTs). Complementarity: the ground can build enormous mirrors cheaply (gravity, cranes, servicing, no mass limit), giving it unbeatable LIGHT-GATHERING for faint objects, and AO gives those giants sharpness too \u2014 but AO cannot un-absorb the blocked spectrum, corrects only a small sky patch, works best in the IR, and doesn't remove sky glow. Space telescopes are penalized by the rocket equation (every gram lifted, launch survival, folded deployment, usually no repair \u2014 JWST cost ~$10B; a 6.5-m space mirror costs more than a 30-m ground one), so space is chosen only when decisive: blocked wavelengths (required) or science needing sharpness/stability/darkness the ground can't match. Hence both persist \u2014 the ground wins light (aperture), space wins the forbidden bands and the most demanding imaging, and AO lets the ground compete on optical/near-IR sharpness. Neither replaces the other; the atmosphere sorts them. The through-line: never lose a photon you don't need to lose \u2014 every technique (climb above the water vapor, escape to vacuum, un-blur with AO, choose dark sites) is a way of not losing precious photons to the great photon-thief, the reward being deep images like the Hubble Deep Field, where ten days on a 'blank' patch of sky returned three thousand galaxies.", "id": "q7"}]$l14_quiz$::jsonb as questions
